  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Areas of fog or low clouds will remain through mid-morning or\n   early afternoon.\n\n-  Light rain or snow is possible today (Thursday) and again on\n   Sunday.\n\nSatellite imagery clearly shows low clouds filling almost all valleys below\n6,000 feet, while the mountains remain completely clear this morning. The\npersistent low clouds may linger through early this afternoon, limiting\nmaximum warming due to strong inversions. For example, Missoula's forecast\nhigh Wednesday was 36F, while the terrain above 6,000 feet warmed into the\nmid 40s to low 50s.\n\nThis afternoon a weak weather system will move across the Northern Rockies,\npushing a weak to moderate cold front through the region. This front will\nincrease westerly winds and mix out the low-level valley inversions. A\nperiod of light valley rain and mountain snow is expected as this system\nmoves through.\n\nHigh pressure immediately rebuilds late Friday, and patchy valley fog or\nlow clouds are possible by Saturday. Depending on how quickly valley\ninversions rebuild, Saturday afternoon could be quite warm for this time of\nyear.\n\nAnother weak weather system will clip the Northern Rockies on Sunday. While\nlight rain or snow is possible in far northwest Montana, the mountains will\nbe breezy with wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph.\n\nHigh pressure rebuilds again to start next week. As valley inversions\nreturn, afternoon highs will slowly cool, and valley fog and low clouds\nwill return each morning.",
